# React Component Patterns

## Structure

- Use functional components only. No class components.
- One component per file; name the file after the component (`UserCard.tsx` → `UserCard`).
- Colocate component-specific types in the same file; shared types go in `src/types/`.
- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks (`useUserProfile`, not 40 lines of logic in JSX).

## Props and composition

- Type props with an explicit interface or type alias.
- Prefer composition over prop drilling — use children and small focused components.
- Avoid boolean prop explosion (`isLoading`, `isError`, `isEmpty`); use discriminated unions or separate components when it grows.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — vague props
function Card({ data, loading }: { data?: User; loading?: boolean }) { ... }

// ✅ GOOD — explicit states
type UserCardProps =
  | { status: "loading" }
  | { status: "error"; message: string }
  | { status: "success"; user: User }
```

## Hooks rules

- Call hooks only at the top level — never inside conditions, loops, or nested functions.
- Keep effects focused; include all dependencies or explain why with an eslint/oxlint disable.
- Prefer derived state over syncing with `useEffect`.

## File layout order

1. Imports (external → internal → types)
2. Types / constants
3. Component
4. Sub-components (if small and private)
5. Default export last

## Avoid

- `useEffect` for data fetching — use TanStack Query instead.
- Index keys in dynamic lists when stable IDs exist.
- Inline arrow functions as default props (creates new references each render).
